    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Optics</span> is the study of light and its interactions with matter. It covers reflection by mirrors, refraction through lenses and prisms, and the formation of images — essential for understanding cameras, spectacles, telescopes, and the human eye.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Reflection at Plane Mirrors</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Laws: angle of incidence = angle of reflection; incident ray, normal, reflected ray are coplanar</li><li>Image in plane mirror: virtual, upright, same size, laterally inverted, same distance behind mirror</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Curved Mirrors</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Concave mirror:</strong> converging; focal point F in front of mirror. Used in: headlights, solar concentrators, dentist mirrors</li><li><strong>Convex mirror:</strong> diverging; focal point F behind mirror. Used in: rear-view mirrors, shop security mirrors — wide field of view</li><li>Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u; magnification m = v/u</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Refraction and Snell's Law</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ (Snell's Law). Refractive index n = c/v = sin i / sin r. Light bends toward the normal when entering a denser medium (lower speed).</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Lenses</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Type</th><th>Image</th><th>Uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Converging (convex)</td><td>Thicker at centre</td><td>Real and inverted (object beyond F); virtual and upright (object within F)</td><td>Camera, projector, magnifying glass, eye</td></tr><tr><td>Diverging (concave)</td><td>Thinner at centre</td><td>Always virtual, upright, diminished</td><td>Correcting short-sightedness (myopia)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"learn-p\">Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u. Power of lens: P = 1/f (unit: dioptre, D). Magnification: m = v/u.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Defects of Vision</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Myopia (short-sightedness):</strong> distant objects blurred; image forms in front of retina. Corrected by diverging (concave) lens.</li><li><strong>Hypermetropia (long-sightedness):</strong> near objects blurred; image would form behind retina. Corrected by converging (convex) lens.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Sign Convention:</strong> Real is positive for image distance (v). Distances measured from optical centre. Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u. Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Snell: n₁sinθ₁=n₂sinθ₂. Converging lens/concave mirror = real image (object beyond F). Myopia → concave lens. Hypermetropia → convex lens. Power = 1/f (D).</div>",
