    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Electrostatics</span> is the study of electric charges at rest. It covers the nature of electric charge, Coulomb's Law, electric fields, electric potential, and capacitance — the foundations of electrical engineering and electronics.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Electric Charge</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Two types: positive (protons) and negative (electrons)</li><li>Like charges repel; unlike charges attract</li><li>Charge is quantised: Q = ne (n = integer; e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)</li><li>Charge is conserved in all processes</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Coulomb's Law</h3><p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>F = kQ₁Q₂/r²</strong></p><p class=\"learn-p\">k = 1/(4πε₀) = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C². Force is along the line joining the charges; attractive for unlike charges, repulsive for like charges.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Electric Field</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Electric field strength E = F/q = kQ/r² (field due to point charge Q). Unit: N/C or V/m. Field lines: from + to −; stronger where lines are closer together.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Electric Potential and Capacitance</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Electric potential:</strong> V = kQ/r. Work done W = QV. Unit: Volt (V = J/C).</li><li><strong>Capacitance:</strong> C = Q/V. Unit: Farad (F). Energy stored: E = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.</li><li>Parallel plate capacitor: C = ε₀A/d (A = plate area, d = separation).</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Coulomb vs Gravity:</strong> F = kQ₁Q₂/r² (Coulomb) vs F = Gm₁m₂/r² (gravity). Same form — inverse square law. k = 9×10⁹; G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹. Gravity always attractive; electrostatic can be attractive or repulsive.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> F=kQ₁Q₂/r². E=kQ/r² (field). V=kQ/r (potential). C=Q/V. Energy=½CV². Charge quantised: Q=ne. Conservation of charge.</div>",
