    "topic": "Current Electricity",
    "topicCode": "SS2-PHY-13",
    "module": "Electricity",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Current electricity</span> deals with the flow of electric charge through conductors. Key concepts include Ohm's Law, resistance, circuit analysis (Kirchhoff's Laws), electrical power, and safety. These form the basis of all electronic and electrical engineering.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Current, Voltage, and Resistance</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Current:</strong> I = Q/t (charge per unit time). Unit: Ampere (A).</li><li><strong>Voltage (EMF/PD):</strong> V = W/Q (work per unit charge). Unit: Volt (V).</li><li><strong>Ohm's Law:</strong> V = IR (for ohmic conductors at constant temperature). Unit of resistance: Ohm (Ω).</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Resistance in Circuits</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Series:</strong> R_total = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ + ... (same current; voltages add)</li><li><strong>Parallel:</strong> 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃ + ... (same voltage; currents add)</li><li>Resistivity: R = ρL/A (depends on material ρ, length L, cross-sectional area A)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Electrical Power and Energy</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>P = IV = I²R = V²/R. Unit: Watt (W)</li><li>E = Pt = VIt. Unit: Joule (J)</li><li>Household energy unit: kilowatt-hour (kWh). 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Kirchhoff's Laws</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>KCL (Junction Rule):</strong> Sum of currents entering a junction = sum leaving (charge conservation)</li><li><strong>KVL (Loop Rule):</strong> Sum of potential differences around any closed loop = zero (energy conservation)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Internal Resistance</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Terminal PD = EMF − Ir = E − Ir. Where I = current drawn, r = internal resistance, E = EMF.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Key:</strong> Series: R_T = R₁+R₂ (currents same). Parallel: 1/R_T = 1/R₁+1/R₂ (voltages same). P = IV = I²R = V²/R. Terminal PD = E − Ir.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> I=Q/t. V=IR (Ohm's). Series: R add. Parallel: 1/R add. P=IV=I²R=V²/R. E=Pt. Terminal PD=E−Ir. KCL: currents at junction. KVL: voltages around loop.</div>",
