    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Electromagnetic induction</span> is the production of an EMF (and current) in a conductor when there is a change in magnetic flux through it. Discovered by Faraday in 1831, it is the principle behind generators, transformers, and inductors — the foundation of modern electrical power systems.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Faraday's Law</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">The induced EMF is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux: <strong>EMF = −dΦ/dt = −N × ΔΦ/Δt</strong> (for a coil of N turns). Unit: Volt (V). The magnitude of induced EMF increases with:</p><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Faster change in flux (faster motion or faster field change)</li><li>Stronger magnetic field (B)</li><li>More turns (N) in the coil</li><li>Larger coil area (A)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Lenz's Law</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">The induced current opposes the change in flux that produced it (negative sign in Faraday's Law). This is a consequence of conservation of energy — if the induced current aided the change, energy would be created from nothing.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The AC Generator (Dynamo)</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A coil rotates in a magnetic field. As flux through the coil changes, an EMF is induced. The output alternates sinusoidally: E = E₀ sin(ωt). Components: rotating coil, permanent magnet, slip rings, brushes.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Transformer</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A transformer changes AC voltage using mutual induction. <strong>V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p = I_p/I_s</strong> (for ideal transformer, 100% efficiency). Step-up: N_s > N_p → V_s > V_p. Step-down: N_s < N_p → V_s < V_p.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Eddy Currents and Inductance</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Eddy currents:</strong> currents induced in bulk conductors by changing flux; cause energy losses (heat) in transformer cores — minimised by using laminated cores</li><li><strong>Inductance:</strong> self-inductance L (henry, H); opposes change in current; EMF = −L × dI/dt</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Transformer ratio:</strong> V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. Step-up: more secondary turns. Power in = Power out (ideal): V_pI_p = V_sI_s.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Faraday: EMF = −NΔΦ/Δt. Lenz: induced current opposes change. Generator: rotating coil in field (slip rings). Transformer: V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. Eddy currents → laminated core. Self-inductance opposes current change.</div>",
